{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3167","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3167","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3167\",\"parent_revision\":\"0ecfe6a81a02729f8a72d222e6c73716e976cf35\",\"root_revision\":\"5140398251d8a869deadb606d35a753ead0d79b4776c8ddfc8d5c762682ceeaa\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nTwo findings surfaced while fixing #3157 and deliberately left out of that change to keep it scoped. Both were flagged independently by the adversarial design review and by the implementing agent.\\n\\nFirst finding. operations.py line 1116 defines _AC_HEADER_RE as a MULTILINE, IGNORECASE regex over caret-hash-hash-space-Acceptance. It is a seventh heading scanner of the same class #3157 eliminated: no entry-block awareness, so a heading-shaped line quoted inside an entry's own content reads as a real heading.\\n\\nIts blast radius is much smaller than the six consumers #3157 migrated. It runs only over item.description, not over a rendered body, and it only raises an advisory warning through _check_ac_overlap rather than feeding a section list or a slice. So it does not lose content and does not mislead a gate. It is worth fixing because it is the last surviving instance of a pattern this codebase has now corrected in seven places across #2956, #2970 and #3157, and because leaving one naive scanner in the file invites the next reader to copy it. Note that #3152's own description contains the prose \\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\", which would trip it.\\n\\nThe fix is presumably to route it through parsing.split_body_sections like the others, or to delete it if the advisory it produces is not worth its own scanner. That choice is a grooming decision, not a foregone conclusion.\\n\\nSecond finding, and the more interesting one. Item #3152 reports three different section counts depending on which layer is asked, and the reason is only partly understood:\\n\\n- the live GitHub issue body has 4 sections: Story, Description, Acceptance Criteria, Context\\n- the resolved agent-managed body, reconstructed by github_sync.render_issue_body from the cached record, has 10 sections\\n- the cache record itself has 9 keys under item.sections\\n\\nThe 10-versus-9 gap is explained: Description is rendered into the body from the item's description field and is not a member of item.sections. That was established while writing #3157's tests, and asserting 9 rather than 10 through the body path would have produced a test that fails on correct code.\\n\\nThe 4-versus-10 gap is explained by design, per ARCHITECTURE.md's GitHub writable records section: work-item issue bodies remain human-owned and are never overwritten, while the authoritative agent-managed head is a Contents record on the dh-content branch. The issue body is not expected to track grooming.\\n\\nWhat has not been established is whether any consumer conflates these three, and what happens when one is read expecting another. A read against the live issue body sees 4 sections and no grooming at all. A gate that checks required sections by name would fail such an item even though its grooming is complete in the authoritative store. Whether any code path actually does that has not been traced.\\n\\nThat question is what this item should settle during grooming. It may turn out that every consumer already reads the correct layer, in which case the finding is that the divergence is safe and should be documented where a reader will meet it. It may turn out that something reads the wrong layer, in which case it is a defect of the same family as #3157 \u2014 a consumer trusting a representation that does not hold what it assumes.\\n\\nContext worth carrying into grooming: this session produced five incorrect diagnoses of #3157 before the correct one, and the recurring cause was treating one layer's output as authoritative for another layer's state. Specifically, backlog_view output was treated as ground truth about storage without ever reading the cache record, whose path is printed in the file_path field of every backlog_view response. This item is the same question asked deliberately rather than stumbled into.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **_ac_header_re is a seventh naive heading scanner, and the three-way section-count divergence between issue body, resolved body and cache is unexplained** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-23, flagged as out of scope during the #3157 fix\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ee09b50d70cafd839470c7075d9ea8beace6862bf458e39b1ca3cce8525a52c7\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQOIHfQ\"}"}